   {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://ryansrolfing.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Specialties"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Knee Pain"}]}
  </script>

  <article class="px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.28em] text-[#c4924a]">
     Knee pain and the leg around it
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-5xl leading-tight md:text-7xl">
     Rolfing for Knee Pain in Folsom
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
     The knee mostly does what the hip and the foot ask of it. When those two are not sharing the work, the knee is often the place that complains.
    </p>
   </div>
   <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      Looking above and below the knee
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      A knee can ache from an old sprain, from years of standing with the thigh turned in, or from a foot that cannot adapt to the ground. Rolfing pays attention to that chain: foot, ankle, calf, thigh, hip, and pelvis. The painful knee is the signal, not always the whole story.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Low back and sciatic-type patterns sometimes belong in the same picture, because the leg and the pelvis share the load of standing and walking.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What a session may include
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Ryan may work around the knee and also with the foot, hip, and pelvis that are steering it. A few steps of walking often tell more than pressing on the sore spot. You should be able to say if the pressure is too much.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      The aim is a leg that can share load. It is not a knee that has been forced straight.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What this work does not do
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing is not orthopedic care. It does not repair a torn ligament, a meniscus, or advanced joint damage, and it is not a substitute for imaging when the knee locks or gives way. Swelling after a twist, or a knee that is red and hot, needs a medical visit first.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      If the ache is recurring and tied to how you stand and walk, structural work may be one useful part of taking care of it. It is not a cure.
     </p>
    </section>
   </div>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl rounded-[2rem] bg-[#545454] p-8 text-white md:p-10">
    <h2 class="text-4xl text-white">
     This work may fit if you notice
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-7 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-white/82">
     <li>
      Knee discomfort that returns with walking, stairs, standing, or long drives.
     </li>
     <li>
      One leg, hip, or foot doing more than the other.
     </li>
     <li>
      An old ankle or hip injury that still seems connected to the knee.
     </li>
     <li>
      Temporary relief from local work, then the same pattern coming back.
     </li>
    </ul>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight">
     Questions people ask
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Can Rolfing help knee pain?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Rolfing may support some people with recurring knee strain by working with the foot, ankle, hip, pelvis, and the way the leg bears weight. It is not medical treatment for ligament tears, meniscus injury, or arthritis.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Will you only work on my knee?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Usually no. The knee is often caught between the hip and the foot. Sessions follow that relationship rather than treating the knee as an isolated part.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Is this a good idea after surgery?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Only with your surgeon’s clearance, and with a clear conversation about what is still healing. Rolfing is not post-operative rehabilitation.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       When should I see a doctor first?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Seek medical care first for a knee that locks, gives way, or swells after an injury, for redness and fever, or for pain that is sudden, severe, or worsening.
      </p>
     </details>
    </div>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
     Knee pain bodywork in Folsom
    </h2>
    <p>
     Ryan’s Rolfing is located at 101 Parkshore Drive, Folsom, CA 95630, and serves clients from Folsom, El Dorado Hills, Shingle Springs, Cameron Park, Placerville, and the greater Sacramento area.
    </p>
